          %h2#introduction_and_survey Introduction and survey
          %p
            These notes concern the generalization of the notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbes"} gerbes
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundles"} principal 2-bundles
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-connections"} principal 2-connections
            and generally of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} principal ∞-bundles
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-connection"} principal ∞-connection
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            modeled on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+supergeometry"} higher supergeometry
            modeled on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifolds"} supermanifolds
          %p
            A key motivation for this comes from applications to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            and the induced
            = succeed "-analog" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+geometry"} spin geometry
            called
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+geometry"} string geometry
          %p
            Since early observations in the 1980s (
            %a{:href => "#Gawedzki87"}> Gawędzki 87
            ) and then more prominently since (
            %a{:href => "FreedWitten99"}> Freed-Witten 99
            ,
            = succeed "))," do
              %a{:href => "#CareyJohnsonMurray02"} Carey-Johnson-Murray 02
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            is mathematically a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+2-bundle+with+connection"} circle 2-bundle with connection
            and that the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+term"} WZW term
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of the 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            with such a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface+holonomy"} surface holonomy
            of this 2-connection, in higher analogy of how the gauge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            term of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charged+particle"} charged particle
            is the line
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundle+with+connection"} circle bundle with connection
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
          %p
            While this is well-explored by now, it has almost exclusively been applied to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string"} bosonic string
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomaly+cancellation"} anomaly cancellation
            between the contributions of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            and of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            (on the
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            and/or in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+theory"} index theory
            gives the fermionic anomaly incarnated as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pfaffian+line+bundle"} Pfaffian line bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            to the anomaly is adjusted such as to cancel the class of this anomaly bundle. The main examples of this are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freed-Witten-Kapustin+anomaly"} Freed-Witten-Kapustin anomaly
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            (incarnated as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
            serving as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+generalized+cohomology"} orientation
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+K-theory"} complex K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+anomaly"} Green-Schwarz anomaly
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            (incarnated as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string%5Ec+structure"} string^c structure
            serving as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+generalized+cohomology"} orientation
            in
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
          %p
            However, since in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            all the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermionic
            contributions are connected by (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"}> local
            \!)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            to the bosonic contributions, it is to be expected that on a more fundamental level there are not two independent contributions – one in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+geometry"} spin geometry
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            arrange into one single structure (“supermultiplet”) in the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+supergeometry"} higher supergeometry
          %p
            By general principles of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            it is clear what
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+supergeometry"} higher supergeometry
            is to be: the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ∞-stacks
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifolds"} supermanifolds
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+super+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth super ∞-groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} principal ∞-bundles
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-connections"} principal ∞-connections
            apply to this (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"}> cohesive
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            as to any other and hence in principle provide all the relevant structure.
          %p
            It remains to work out more examples and applications. A good testing ground is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            approximation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+super+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth super ∞-groupoids
            by their
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} super L-∞ algebras
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#SSS09"} SSS 09
            %em implicitly
            been recognized and used in the study of higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            for a long time, starting with (
            %a{:href => "#DAuriaFre80"}> D’Auria-Fré-Regge 80
            ) and fully developed in (
            %a{:href => "CastellaniDAuriaFre91"}> Castellani-D’Auria-Fré 91
            ), namely in their dual incarnation via their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebras"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebras
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Nieuwenhuizen83"} Nieuwenhuizen 83
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+space"} super vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebra"} Grassmann algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            on these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebras"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebras
            is crucially
            %em not
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+construction"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            literature, see the references at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fr%C3%A9+formulation+of+supergravity"} D'Auria-Fré formulation of supergravity
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+action+functional"} Green-Schwarz action functional
          %p
            If however one does make the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory+of+L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} homotopy theory of L-∞ algebras
            explicit that is hidden in the “FDA”-formulation of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Wess-Zumino-Witten+theory"} higher WZW terms
            all along (
            %a{:href => "#FSS13b"}> FSS 13b
            ), namely in form of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+action+functionals"} Green-Schwarz action functionals
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
            of higher-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            propagating in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+spacetime"} super spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#FSS13b"} FSS 13b, last section
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbative
            action functionals formulated (implicitly) in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} super L-∞ algebraic data
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integrates
            to a genuine (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+field+theory"}> non-perturbative
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Wess-Zumino-Witten+theory"} ∞-WZW model
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+supergeometry"} higher supergeometry
          %p
            The present notes are aimed at spelling out class of examples of “super
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-gerbes"} ∞-gerbes
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            We start with traditional basics, first introducing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifolds"} supermanifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin group
            and the classification of its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+representations"} spin representations
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetimes"} super Minkowski spacetimes
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+spaces"} coset spaces
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} super Poincaré group
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetries"} supersymmetries
          %p
            Then we introduce
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+supergeometry"} higher supergeometry
            in terms of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+stacks"} super stacks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+super+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth super ∞-groupoids
            and finally discuss how all the exceptional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+cocycles"} L-∞ cocycles
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
            and its higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+extensions"} L-∞ extensions
            yield the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+sigma+models"} Green-Schwarz sigma models
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+scan"} brane scan
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/The+brane+bouquet"} brane bouquet
            of branes with “tensor multiplet” fields, such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            and notably the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
          %h2#1_superpoints_and_supermanifolds
            %strong 1)
            Superpoints and supermanifolds
          %h3#summary Summary
          %p
            Where ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            is modeled on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^d
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            between them,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            is modeled on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Cartesian+spaces"} super Cartesian spaces
            that are denoted
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{p|q}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p,q \in \mathbb{N}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{0|q}
            are characterized by the fact that their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebra"} function algebra
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebra"} Grassmann algebra
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators"} generators
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifold"} supermanifold
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p|q)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            locally modeled on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{p|q}
          %p
            Introductions to traditional discussion of supermanifolds include (
            %a{:href => "#Varadarajan04"}> Varadarajan 04, chapter 4
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#HohnholdStolzTeichner11"} Hohnhold-Stolz-Teichner 11
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Riemann+surfaces"} super Riemann surfaces
            and with an emphasis on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+over+supermanifolds"} integration over supermanifolds
            is in (
            %a{:href => "#Witten12"}> Witten 12
            ). See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signs+in+supergeometry"} signs in supergeometry
          %p
            In some accounts of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#supermanifold#DeWitt"} DeWitt 92
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebra"} Grassmann algebra
            to model the idea that one can arbitrarily probe any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifold"} supermanifold
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
          %p
            That fixing such an algebra is unnatural, and that the natural formulation rather is to probe by
            %em all
            finite-dimensional Grassmann algebras/superpoints in a way that is respects “change of odd coordinates” was realized in 1984 by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Schwarz"} Albert Schwarz
            and others, see (
            %a{:href => "#KonechnySchwarz98"}> Konechny-Schwarz 98, appendix
            ) for a review. In more abstract language this insight says that supergeometry happens in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+infinity-groupoid"} topos over superpoints
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+supergeometry"} higher supergeometry
            that we come to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#HigherSupergeometry"} below
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos-theoretic
            perspective on supergeometry can be further enhanced by invoking the supergeometric analog of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+supergeometry"} synthetic differential supergeometry
            is developed in (
            %a{:href => "#CarchediRoytenberg12"}> Carchedi-Roytenberg 12
            ).
          %h2#SuperLieAlgebrasAndSuperLieGroups
            %strong 2)
            Super Lie algebras and super Lie groups
          %h3#summary_2 Summary
          %p
            By
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalization
            all the standard notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            are implemented in the super-context to yield
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            in the super context.
          %p
            By the above
            = succeed "-perspective," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+infinity-groupoid"} super-topos
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi SuperLieAlg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} \in SuperLieAlg
            is a collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mi q
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi even
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi LieAlg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathfrak{g}\otimes \Lambda^q)_{even} \in LieAlg
            of ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebra"} Grassmann algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi even
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi even
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathfrak{g}\otimes \Lambda^{q_2})_{even} \longrightarrow (\mathfrak{g}\otimes \Lambda^{q_1})_{even}
            for each change of Grassmann coordinates
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 2
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^{q_2} \longrightarrow \Lambda^{q_1}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            of ordinary Lie algebras over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            this is equivalently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+space"} super vector space
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+bracket"} Lie bracket
            which is
            %em symmetric
            on two odd-graded elements and skew-symmetric otherwise, and which satisfies a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+identity"} Jacobi identity
            with signs depending suitably on the degree of the elements.
          %p
            In precisely the same fashion one finds all super-algebraic structures such as for instance also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} super L-∞ algebras
            %a{:href => "#HigherSupergeometry"} below
            in the discussion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+supergeometry"} higher supergeometry
          %p
            Similarly a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+group"} super Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is just a system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(\mathbb{R}^{0|q})
            of ordinary Lie groups, equipped with compatible Lie group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(\mathbb{R}^{0|q_2}) \longrightarrow G(\mathbb{R}^{0|q_1})
            for each algebra homomorphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 2
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^{q_2} \longrightarrow \Lambda^{q_1}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
          %p
            A decent exposition of this general principle of super Lie algebras and super Lie groups is for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#Varadarajan04"}> Varadarajan 04, chapter 7.1
            ). Discussion of the classical examples is in (
            %a{:href => "#Varadarajan04"}> Varadarajan 04, chapter 7.3
            ).
          %h2#SpinGroupAndSpinRepresentations
            %strong 3)
            Spin group and spin representations
          %h3#history History
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebra"}> Clifford algebra
            s, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin group
            and its
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+function"} wave function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi \colon \mathbb{R}^4 \to \mathbb{C}
            (we choose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            to be the time coordinate). The dynamics of the system obeys the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+equation"} Schrödinger equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mn 0
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %mi H
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  i\partial_0\psi	= H\,\psi
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton+operator"} Hamilton operator
            measuring the energy. Now let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_i
            be the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            be the mass of the particle. Note that the above equation is of first order whereas the relativistic energy condition,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mn 3
                    %msubsup
                      %mi P
                      %mi i
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi m
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^2 = \sum_{i=1}^3 P_i^2 + m^2
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+equation"} Schrödinger equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mn 0
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi j
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi m
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (i\partial_0)^2\psi = H^2\,\psi = \left[ \sum_{j=1}^3 (-i\partial_j)^2 + m^2 \right]\,\psi
          %p
            The resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+equation"} Klein-Gordon equation
            describes the kinematics of spinless scalar particles. Dirac asked the question, whether it is possible to write down a relativistically covariant first order equation. The ansatz
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mn 3
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi j
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H = \sum_{j=1}^3 \alpha_j\,(-i\partial_j) + \alpha_0\,m
          %p
            yields the conditions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi j
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi jk
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_j\alpha_k + \alpha_k\alpha_j = \delta_{jk}\,1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_j
            are complex numbers. Dirac’s conclusion was that the particle had some inner degree of freedom - the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
            \- which forces the wave functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            to be vector-valued and transforming under a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            of the above algebra, which was already known as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebra"} Clifford algebra
          %h3#clifford_algebras Clifford algebras
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a finite dimensional vector space over a field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            of characteristic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and denote by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            a non-degenerate quadratic form on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q(v) = \phi(v,v)
            for some non-degenerate symmetric bilinear form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon V \otimes V \to k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,Q)
            will be called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em quadratic vector space
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The
              %em Clifford algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(V,Q)
              associated to a quadratic vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,Q)
              is the quotient of the tensor algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(V) = \oplus_{r \geq 0} V^{\otimes r}
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              by the ideal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              generated by elements of the form
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi y
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mn 2
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_{x,y} = x \otimes y + y \otimes x - 2\phi(x,y)\cdot 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is the ideal generated by elements
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \otimes x - Q(x) \cdot 1
          %p
            The tensor algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(V)
            is
            = succeed "-graded." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is not homogeneous, this grading does not descend to the quotient. However,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(V,Q)
            is still
            = succeed "-graded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}
            %em filtered
            by the length of tensors. This filtration leads to an important connection between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebra"} Clifford algebra
            associated to a quadratic vector space and its
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+algebra"} exterior algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi gr
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi Λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Cl(V,Q)^{gr} \cong \Lambda(V)
          %p
            An isomorphism is induced by the linear map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \colon \Lambda(V) \to Cl(V)
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi …
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo !
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mi r
                    %mi sign
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi …
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi r
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda(v_1 \wedge \dots \wedge v_r) = \frac{1}{r!}\sum_{\sigma \in \Sigma_r} sign(\sigma)v_{\sigma(1)}\,\dots\,v_{\sigma(r)}
          %p
            The following theorem is the key to all structural results about
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebra"} Clifford algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebras"} superalgebras
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,Q)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V',Q')
              be two quadratic vector spaces. Then
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Cl
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Q
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⊕
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ′
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Q
                            %mo ′
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≅
                            %mi Cl
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Q
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi Cl
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ′
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Q
                            %mo ′
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Cl
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mi Q
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≅
                            %mi Cl
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Q
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    Cl((V,Q) \oplus (V',Q')) &amp; \cong Cl(V,Q) \otimes Cl(V',Q') \\
                    Cl(V,-Q) &amp; \cong Cl(V,Q)^{op}
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
              Moreover, we have
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi dim
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %mi dim
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dim(Cl(V,Q)) = 2^{\dim(V)}
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              We drop the quadratic forms from the notation and just sketch the proof. The
              = succeed "-linear" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi V
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \colon V \oplus V' \to Cl(V) \otimes Cl(V)
              satisfies
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo ,
                      %mi v
                      %mo ′
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi Q
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(v,v')^2 = Q(v)\,1 + Q'(v')\,1
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi V
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(V \oplus V') \to Cl(V) \otimes Cl(V')
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi V
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(V) \to Cl(V \oplus V')
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi V
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(V') \to Cl(V \oplus V')
              yield
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi V
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(V) \otimes Cl(V') \to Cl(V \oplus V')
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(V,Q)
            %p
              For the second statement consider the homomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(V) \to Cl(V,Q)^{op}
              sending
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi …
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 \otimes \dots \otimes x_k
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi …
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x_k \otimes \dots \otimes x_1]
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \otimes x + Q(x)\,1
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              and the kernel is generated by these elements. Therefore it descends to a surjective map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(V,-Q) \to Cl(V,Q)
          %p
            From this we can completely classify all complex Clifford algebras, i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(V,Q)
            for a complex vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                    %msubsup
                      %mi z
                      %mi i
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q(z) = \sum_{j=1}^n z_i^2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(V,Q)
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}l(V)
            in this case.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is a complex vector space with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi dim
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dim(V) = 2m
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}l(V) \cong End(S)
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi dim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dim(S) = 2^{m-1}|2^{m-1}
              as graded algebras.
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is a complex vector space with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi dim
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dim(V) = 2m+1
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo +
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}l(V) \cong \mathbb{C}l(V)^+ \otimes D
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = \mathbb{C}[\epsilon] \cong \mathbb{C}l(\mathbb{C}^1)
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon^2 = 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}l(V)^+ \cong End(S_0)
              where
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi dim
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dim(S_0) = 2^m
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              We can check directly that
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}l(\mathbb{C}^2) \cong M_2(\mathbb{C})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_1, e_2
              be two orthogonal basis vectors of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ↦
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mrow
                          %mtable{:displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mn 0
                              %mtd
                                %mn 1
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mn 1
                              %mtd
                                %mn 0
                        %mo )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_1 \mapsto \begin{pmatrix}0 &amp; 1 \\ 1 &amp; 0 \end{pmatrix}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ↦
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mrow
                        %mtable{:displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mn 0
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 1
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mn 1
                            %mtd
                              %mn 0
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_2 \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; -1 \\ 1 &amp; 0 \end{pmatrix}
              defines an isomorphism of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebras"} superalgebras
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi m
                          %mo −
                          %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}l(\mathbb{C}^{2m}) \cong \mathbb{C}l(\mathbb{C}^{2m-2}) \otimes \mathbb{C}l(\mathbb{C}^{2})
          %p
            From the above theorem we deduce that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mi l
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}l(V)
            has exactly two (graded) simple modules
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi S
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with the reversed grading) in the even-dimensional case and exactly one graded simple module
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = S_0 \otimes D
            in case the dimension is odd.
          %p
            The classification of Clifford algebras is a little more intricate in the case of real vector spaces. Here we can without loss of generality assume that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+space"} super vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{s|t}
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
              is called a
              %em super division algebra
              if all nonzero homogeneous elements are invertible. A real
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is called
              %em central simple
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi ℝ
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes_{\mathbb{R}} \mathbb{C} \cong M_{s|t}(\mathbb{C})
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi ℝ
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi ℂ
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes_{\mathbb{R}} \mathbb{C} \cong M_n(\mathbb{C}) \otimes_{\mathbb{C}} D
              (with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              as above) as superalgebras. We will write
              %em CS superalgebra
              for short.
          %p
            There are a lot of different characterizations of CS
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebras"} superalgebras
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Varadarajan04"} Varadarajan 04, Theorem 6.2.5
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
            over a field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            of characteristic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is isomorphic to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{s|t}(k) \otimes K
            for some super division algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            such that its supercenter is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Two CS
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebras"} superalgebras
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_2
              are called
              %em similar
              if their associated super division algebras are isomorphic, i.e. if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi s
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %msub
                          %mi t
                          %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_i \cong M_{s_i|t_i}(k) \otimes K
              for some super division algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebras"} superalgebras
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi sBr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sBr(k)
              with respect to the graded tensor product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes_k
              turns out to be a group and is called the
              %em super Brauer group
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            The following is proven in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Varadarajan04"} Varadarajan 04, Theorem 6.4.1
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              The super Brauer group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sBr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sBr(\mathbb{R})
              is isomorphic to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 8
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/8\mathbb{Z}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi ℝ
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{\mathbb{R}}=\mathbb{R}[\epsilon]
              be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
              odd and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon^2 = 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,Q)
              is a quadratic vector space, then
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mi ℝ
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mrow
                          %mi sign
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Q
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [Cl(V,Q)] = [D_{\mathbb{R}}]^{sign(Q)}
          %p
            This result explains the
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 8
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 8
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V= \mathbb{R}^{s|t}
            with dimension
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo =
                    %mi s
                    %mo +
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = s+t
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
