            is the 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+space"} complex vector space
            on which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"} quaternions
            naturally act.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The last column implies that in each dimension there exists a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Gamma \;\colon\; S^\ast \otimes S^\ast \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^{d-1,1}
            %p which is
            %ol
              %li
                %p symmetric;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Spin
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin(V)
                  \-equivariant.
            %p
              This allows to form the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
              in each of these cases. See there and see
              %em
                %a{:href => "#SpinorBilinearForms"} Spinor bilinear forms
              below for more.
          %h2#SuperMinkowskiSpacetime
            %strong 4)
            Super Poincaré group and super Minkowski spacetime
          %h3#summary_4 Summary
          %p
            Ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{d-1,1}
            happens to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+space"} coset space
            which is the quotient of (the double cover of) its own Lie group of oriented
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometries"} isometries
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+group"} Poincaré group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(d-1,1)
            by (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+cover"} double cover
            of) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(d-1,1)
            of rotations and boosts:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1} \simeq Iso(d-1,1)/SO(d-1,1)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The same is true already for the corresponding
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi 𝔦𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1} \simeq \mathfrak{iso}(d-1,1)/\mathfrak{so}(d-1,1)
                  \\,.
          %p
            While for ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            this identification is maybe not very deep, for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            it becomes crucial:
          %p
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+extension"} extension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{siso}_N(d-1,1)
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} Poincaré Lie algebra
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+representation"} spin representation
            in odd degree – a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
            – one
            %em defines
            the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
            as the quotient
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ;
                      %mi N
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}^{d;N} \coloneqq \mathfrak{siso}_N(d-1,1)/\mathfrak{so}(d-1,1)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Good mathematical discussion of this construction is in (
            %a{:href => "#DeligneFreed99"}> Deligne-Freed 99, section 1.1
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Freed99"} Freed 99, lecture 3
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Varadarajan04"} Varadarajan 04, chapter 7
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is in (
            %a{:href => "#Polchinski01"}> Polchinski 01, volume II, appendix B
            ).
          %p
            Since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+Lie+algebra"} special orthogonal Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{so}(d-1,1)
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+sub+Lie+algebra"} normal
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔦𝔰𝔬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{iso}(d-1,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
            itself as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+translation+Lie+algebra"} super translation Lie algebra
            over itself. Accordingly one can ask for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} Lie algebra cohomology
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/translation+Lie+algebra"} translation Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            from above – happen to admit a finite number of exceptional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} Lie cocycles
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+scan"} brane scan
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AzcarragaIzqierdo95"} Azcárraga-Izqierdo 95
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CAIP99"} Chrysso‌malakos-Azcárraga-Izquierdo-Bueno 99
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg
            super-dg-algebra of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
            goes back to (
            %a{:href => "#Nieuwenhuizen83"}> Nieuwenhuizen 83
            ).
          %p
            Since by above,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
            is defined as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+space"} coset space
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanics"} mechanics
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            and more generally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            propagating in super Minkowski spacetimes are naturally given by super-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+models"}> coset models
            \. On the Lie-algebraic level this is discussed nicely in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AzcarragaIzqierdo95"} Azcarraga-Izqierdo 95, section 8
            %em curved
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+spacetime"} super spacetime
            is most naturally thought of in terms of super-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"}> Cartan geometry
            \. This is (somewhat implicitly) the approach to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            in (
            %a{:href => "#CastellaniDAuriaFre91"}> Castellani-D’Auria-Fré
            ).
          %h2#HigherSupergeometry
            %strong 5)
            Higher supergeometry
          %h3#summary_5 Summary
          %p
            In order to pass from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+supergeometry"} higher supergeometry
          %p
            Above we already mentioned that ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            takes place in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi SuperPoints
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(SuperPoints)
            of “super sets”. In order to model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} super ∞-groupoids
            we hence naturally pass to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            such as to yield
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+super+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth super ∞-groupoids
            we form the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaf+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-sheaf (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothSuper%E2%88%9EGrpd"} SmoothSuper∞Grpd
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mn ∞
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mrow
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi q
                        %mo }
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ,
                        %mi q
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                    %mo )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mn ∞
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi SuperManifolds
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coloneqq Sh_\infty\left(\left\{\mathbb{R}^{p|q}\right\}_{p,q \in \mathbb{N}}\right) \simeq Sh_\infty\left(SuperManifolds\right)
          %p
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifolds"} supermanifolds
            (see for instance
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FSS13a"} FSS 13a
          %p
            As in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} principal ∞-bundles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} associated ∞-bundles
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-gerbes"} ∞-gerbes
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothSuper%E2%88%9EGrpd"} SmoothSuper∞Grpd
            (
            %a{:href => "#NSS12"}> NSS 12
            ). The basic fact of relevance here is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothSuper%E2%88%9EGrpd"} SmoothSuper∞Grpd
            is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive
            (
            %a{:href => "#S"}> S
            ) and hence also admits refinements of these structures to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
          %p
            In particular, due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+%E2%88%9E-group"} super ∞-group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Grp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi SmoothSuper
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \in Grp(SmoothSuper\infty Grpd)
            naturally has a canonical higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maurer-Cartan+form"} Maurer-Cartan form
            exhibited by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mo ♭
                    %mi dR
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\theta_G \;\colon\; G \longrightarrow \flat_{dR}\mathbf{B}G
          %p
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in non-abelian de Rham hypercohomology. Since the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+gerbe"} WZW gerbe
            is characterized by a 3-cocycle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} Lie algebra cohomology
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi cl
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(\theta_Q) \colon G \to \Omega^3_{cl}
            is its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            3-form, this is a crucial ingredient for the definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Wess-Zumino-Witten+theory"} ∞-Wess-Zumino-Witten theory
            models. This we come to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#SuperWZWBundles"} below
          %h2#SuperWZWBundles
            %strong 6)
            Super Wess-Zumino-Witten gerbes /
            = succeed "-connections" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            We discuss how to construct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundles+with+connection"} circle n-bundles with connection
            (“bundle (n-1)-gerbes”) on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+groups"} super Lie groups
            and on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+super+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} super ∞-groups
            which generalize the familiar
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} Wess-Zumino-Witten term
            that serves as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            that describes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            propagating on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Wess-Zumino-Witten+theory"} ∞-Wess-Zumino-Witten theories
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theories"} boundary field theories
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theories"} ∞-Chern-Simons theories
            (which in turn are realized as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theories"} boundary field theories
            for higher
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+Yang-Mills+theories"} topological Yang-Mills theories
          %p
            Finally we apply this general construction of supergeometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Wess-Zumino-Witten+theory"} ∞-Wess-Zumino-Witten theories
            to the exceptional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} super L-∞ algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+supersymmetry"} extended supersymmetry
            algebras and thus find the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+field+theory"} non-pertrubative
            formulation of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            which constitute the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+scan"} brane scan
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/The+brane+bouquet"} The brane bouquet
          %h3#introduction Introduction
          %p
            A
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
            is traditionally defined by an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Fields
                    %mi traj
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Fields}_{traj}
            “of
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi Fields
                            %mi traj
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi exp
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi i
                                %mi S
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\mathbf{Fields}_{traj}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\exp(i S)}}
                  \\\
                  U(1)
                  }
          %p
            to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            from this is to
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                choose a linearization in the form of the group
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⟶
                      %msub
                        %mi GL
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1) \longrightarrow GL_1(\mathbb{C})
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+units"} group of units
                of the complex numbers,
            %li
              %p
                choose a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\mu
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi Fields
                        %mi traj
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Fields}_{traj}
          %p
            and then declare that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"}> path integral
            ”)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi Fields
                        %mi traj
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi d
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underset{\phi \in \mathbf{Fields}_{traj}}{\int} \exp(i S(\phi))\, d\mu \in \mathbb{C}
          %p
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of the theory a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+value"} expectation value
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probabilities"} probabilities
            replaced by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitudes"} probability amplitudes
          %p
            In order to make sense of this (for a full discussion of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"}> motivic quantization
            ” in this sense see (
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten13"}> Nuiten 13
            ), here we concentrate on the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} pre-quantum aspects
            = succeed "." do
